---
title: Use Nx Behind a Corporate Proxy
description: Learn how to configure proxy environment variables so Nx can reach Nx Cloud through a corporate proxy, including custom certificate setup.
filter: 'type:Guides'
topics:
  - 'Troubleshooting'
---

Nx makes a small number of network requests, such as downloading the Nx Cloud client and connecting your workspace to Nx Cloud. On networks that route outbound traffic through a proxy, configure the standard proxy environment variables and Nx picks them up automatically.

## Configure proxy environment variables

Set `HTTPS_PROXY` (and `HTTP_PROXY` for plain HTTP traffic) to your proxy URL:

```shell
export HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy.example.com:8080
```

If your proxy requires credentials, include them in the URL:

```shell
export HTTPS_PROXY=http://user:password@proxy.example.com:8080
```

To exclude specific hosts from proxying, list them in `NO_PROXY`:

```shell
export NO_PROXY=internal.example.com,localhost
```

## Use custom certificates

If your proxy performs TLS interception with a private certificate authority, point Node.js at the CA bundle with [`NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`](https://nodejs.org/api/cli.html#node_extra_ca_certsfile):

```shell
export NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/path/to/company-ca.pem
```

## Set the variables on CI

Set the same environment variables in your CI provider's configuration so agents behind the proxy can reach Nx Cloud. Most providers offer a way to define environment variables for all pipeline steps, such as the `env` block in GitHub Actions workflows.
